.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-SET-EGRESS-LIMIT 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-set-egress-limit \- set how fast all applications together may call one outside site
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eap set-egress-limit
\fB\-\-site\fR \fIhost\fR
[\fB\-\-requests\-per\-second\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-burst\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-concurrent\fR \fIn\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the egress limit for one outside site: how many requests per
second, and how many at once, euclid's applications may make to it -
all instances of all applications on all nodes counted together.
.PP
A site that protects itself against denial of service counts per source
address, and everything euclid runs reaches it from the same one. A
limit kept inside an application counts per instance, which the
autoscaler multiplies exactly when there's the most to fetch. So the
limit is kept in EAP, once per site for the whole installation, and an
application asks for a permit before each request it makes to the site
- with the euclid-jdk
.BR EuclidEgressLimiter :
.PP
.RS
.nf
try (var permit = limiter.acquire(uri.getHost())) {
    response = http.send(request, ...);
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A permit is a token from the site's bucket (the rate) and a slot among
its requests in flight (the concurrency). The slot is held until the
permit is closed, or until its lease runs out if the instance holding
it dies. When the site answers 429 or 503, the application reports it,
and no permit is granted for that site to anyone until the site's
.I Retry-After
has passed (at most an hour per report).
.PP
Only applications that ask are held to the limit; euclid does not
intercept outgoing traffic.
.PP
A site without its own limit gets
.IR euclid.modules.eap.egress.default :
.PP
.RS
.nf
"egress": { "default": { "requests-per-second": 2, "burst": 2, "concurrent": 4 } }
.fi
.RE
.PP
The limit set here replaces that default for the one site. It applies to
every EAP instance at once and survives a restart. The caller must be
an administrator.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fIhost\fR
The site's host name, e.g. \fIdownloads.example.com\fR. Case and a
trailing dot don't matter; the port and path aren't part of it.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-requests\-per\-second " " \fIn\fR
Requests per second, averaged, for all applications together. May be a
fraction: \fI0.5\fR is one every two seconds. \fI0\fR for no rate limit.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-burst " " \fIn\fR
How many requests may go in quick succession before the rate applies;
at least 1.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrent " " \fIn\fR
Requests to the site in flight at once. \fI0\fR for no limit.
.PP
At least one is required; those not given keep what is in force.
.SH OUTPUT
The site and the limits now in force for it, as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLE
One download at a time, at most one start every two seconds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap set-egress-limit --site downloads.example.com \e
    --requests-per-second 0.5 --burst 1 --concurrent 1
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-egress-sites (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-egress-limit (1)
